# {{.Name}}

A lore project for [lorekeep](https://github.com/gmreyer/lorekeep). Each entity
— a character, a place, an event — is one Markdown file with YAML frontmatter
under `world/`. The frontmatter is the graph; the prose below it is yours.

## Building

From this folder:

```
lorekeep.exe build .
```

`build` checks the whole world and, if it is sound, writes `build/index.db` and
`build/snapshot.json`. **Nothing is written when there are errors.** It exits:

- `0` — the world is valid and the build was written.
- `1` — the world has errors; nothing was written. Read the findings.
- `2` — lorekeep could not read the project (no `schema/` or no `world/`).

Errors are things that make the graph wrong: a reference to an id that does not
exist, a relation or entity type nobody declared. Warnings are judgement calls
and never stop a build.

## Layout

```
schema/            this world's vocabulary, on top of lorekeep's core pack
  pack.yaml            name, version, core_version
  entity-types.yaml, relations.yaml, eras.yaml, acts.yaml
world/             authored lore, one entity per file, grouped by type
lorekeep-version   the lorekeep version this project is built with
build/             derived output, safe to delete
```

The project pack starts empty on purpose. The core pack already provides the
structural entity types and relations; add your own only when writing needs
them.

Two version pins, two different facts: `lorekeep-version` says which lorekeep
builds this project; `core_version` in `schema/pack.yaml` says which core
vocabulary the lore was written against. `lorekeep build` reports when they
drift.

## Git

A project works without git. If you do track it with git, keep the clone out
of OneDrive: Windows points `Documents` at OneDrive by default, and a synced
repository produces file locks and phantom conflicts. `.gitattributes` keeps
line endings as LF so that every diff shows only what changed, and
`world/.gitkeep` keeps `world/` present in a fresh clone even when it is empty.
